<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  What the FileProvider is allowed to expose.

  Exactly one directory: the one holding verified, complete files. Never the whole cache, never
  external storage, never the in-flight staging directory. A FileProvider rooted at "." would let
  any path traversal that got past the filename sanitiser turn into a readable URI for arbitrary
  app-private data, so the root is kept as narrow as the feature allows.

  The path must be the FULL path under cacheDir, including the "relay_cache" container:
  ContentCache puts generic received files in <cacheDir>/relay_cache/received/. This said "ready/"
  for a while, which resolves to <cacheDir>/ready/ and matches nothing, so the first real
  getUriForFile call threw IllegalArgumentException and took the app down. It went unnoticed
  because nothing exercised the provider until the received-files actions existed. Keep it in step
  with ContentCache.DIRECTORY_NAME and RECEIVED_DIR; FileProviderPathTest fails if it drifts.

  Three sibling directories are deliberately absent:

    * "incoming/" holds partials. Unverified: their hash has not been checked, so their bytes are
      whatever the peer sent. Granting a URI to one would hand another app a half-written file
      that failed integrity checking, and nothing here needs to read a partial back through a
      content URI — the receiver holds its own File handle while writing.
    * "presentation/" holds images and PDFs shown on screen. The user never asked to keep or
      share those, and they are not part of the received-files feature.
    * "spool/" holds outbound copies of the user's own documents, waiting to be sent. Nothing
      outside this process has any reason to read them.
-->
<paths>
    <!-- Verified generic files the user received. The only thing shareable. -->
    <cache-path name="received" path="relay_cache/received/" />
</paths>
